      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3f24b968-c90a-5088-b77c-2d14ff47db8f",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{8}=\\cos\\theta,\\quad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8} & =\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(8)^{2}} & =\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & =\\cos^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2)^{2}} & =\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\sin^2\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
